@page "/diff"

<CatHead Title="Diffs">
    Two versions of a text, line by line: a configuration before and after a change, a template, a
    script. Your app computes the diff and writes the rows; the classes draw them. A word that changed
    in running text needs none of this — <code>&lt;ins&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;del&gt;</code> mark it
    on their own (<a href="/prose">Prose</a>).
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Diff.Unified" Title="A unified diff" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.diff</code> around a <code>.diff-head</code> you write and a <code>.diff-body</code> holding a
        <code>table.diff-table</code>: per line the old number, the new number and the text, each
        <code>.diff-num</code> or <code>.diff-code</code>. <code>.diff-add</code> and <code>.diff-del</code> on
        a row mark it added or removed; a context row carries neither. Every changed line draws a + or −
        before its text, so the change never rests on its tint, and neither the marker nor the numbers are
        copied with the code. Inside a line, <code>&lt;ins&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;del&gt;</code> mark the
        words that changed.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>.diff-fold</code> is a row for lines left out, with your own button that loads them — the
        library loads nothing. Give the table a visually hidden caption and header row, as here, so a
        screen reader can say which column is which. The lines are code: they scroll sideways rather
        than wrap, and stay left to right on a right-to-left page.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Diff.Split" Title="Side by side" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.diff--split</code> puts the old text beside the new: four cells a row, the old number and
        line, then the new. The state goes on the cells — <code>.diff-del</code> on the old pair,
        <code>.diff-add</code> on the new — and a side with no line in that row is <code>.diff-gap</code>.
        One row holds both halves of a change, so they stay level however either wraps; long lines wrap,
        because two halves cannot scroll as one, and the body scrolls once a half would be narrower than
        <code>--diff-min</code>.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.Diff.Wrap" Title="Wrapped lines" Demo="ex-demo--block">
    <p>
        <code>.diff--wrap</code> wraps a unified diff's long lines under themselves instead of scrolling —
        right for a command line or a sentence, where seeing the whole change matters more than the
        columns. The marker and the line numbers stay on the first line of the row.
    </p>
</CatExample>
